u want to understand  is this code-based smelly you have to  understand what is the scope  i need to examine but from the  perspective of what we talk about in  this video and in the book as well from  a  cognitive perspective these different  scopes of code smells also really do  different things to your brain and  that's what we're gonna zoom into  a little bit more in this talk so first  thing let's examine  in general like later in the video we'll  talk about specific examples of code  smells and why they are  troublesome from a cognitive perspective  but let's first look at  code smells in general like why are code  smells  problematic in general and to answer  that question we have to  go back to those three different types  of cognitive loads what code smells  do is they increase extraneous load  right so the the lowest level the  intrinsic load still stays the same  if you have a function that calculates  uh i don't know it calculates the square  or something it doesn't really matter if  you do this in one line or if you do  this in a thousand lines  for the inherent cognitive load for the  complexity of the problem itself it  doesn't really matter how you write it  but the way you phrase it is very much  gonna increase  potentially the extraneous cognitive  load the  extra effort that your brain has to  put in to understand the code so  extraneous load  is the part of cognitive loads where  code smells  create issues so diving a little bit  deeper in  different smells and different cognitive  issues because  not all smells are smelly for the same  cognitive reasons and for that  also we have to do a little recap of  stuff that we talked about in a previous  video  and stuff that i talked about earlier in  the book is  what exactly happens in your brain what  happens in your brain if you process  like anything  specifically if you process code you  might remember from the previous video  that there are actually  three different types of memory systems  involved firstly if code comes into your  brain like through your eyes and your  senses  code firstly comes into the short term  memory so you read a bit of code and  first it's only  remembered in the short-term memory then  thinking about code processing the code  actually happens in the working memory  that's the the box in the middle and  the lower end so you have working memory  where the code is being processed  while you are thinking about code your  long-term memory  also sends information to your working  memory  so you're not of course reading ac  program like you've never seen  any c or any program before your  long-term memory says hey  here's the semantics of an if statement  and this is what curly brackets mean and  this is what the meaning of that  variable name is so your long-term  memory  is sort of shooting off information to  your working memory like  hey here's some relevant things that you  might also want to consider while  thinking about code this is more or less  what happens in your brain  and we will dive now into what code  smells  sort of mess up in the arrows that are  there  in your brain when you're looking at  code  if we have a smell like a long parameter  list  we have a method that rather than taking  in two or three parameters takes in  12 parameters or something so here's an  example  maybe you have seen some something like  this before this is like a  constructor type of function that takes  in lots of data and then  puts all the arguments on uh on the  object so you see that this list of  parameters is very very long so if you  want to properly  call this method you look at the  definition like  okay what goes in oh country state  city place date average max min  pollutant okay this all goes in so if  you want to call this method  you look at the definition and then you  go to the location of your code where  you want to actually  create an instance of this class you  have to remember  excuse me you have to remember  everything that was there and of course  in some situations ides can help you a  bit there  by also looking at the code and giving  you a list of what is there  but not always and not always enough  so a long parameter list creates an  issue in your short short-term memory  it isn't necessarily that this code is  hard to process  it isn't necessarily that your long-term  memory doesn't know what's going on  it's not it's not hard to understand  right it's not hard to remember what's  going on  the thing that is hard is remembering  everything  and i'm sure you've been in this  situation where you've looked at a  certain  api definition or a definition of a  function  you're like okay so looking at the api  reference okay let's go  so what is there okay this is this in  this order  and then you hop over to your actual  code where you're going to do the api  call and like okay  oh i forgot what was it again you have  to go back to  the definition definition in the code  you have to go go back to  your api rather than oh yeah this was  the order and that sometimes goes like  one or two or three times  which is just like time and energy  wasted  so that is very much a short-term memory  issue  that's why you don't want to create  calls you don't want to create  functions with very long parameter lists  because then you're putting  the burden of remembering everything and  remembering everything in the right  order  on the color of the method so long  parameter list is troublesome because it  overloads the short term memory people  we've talked about this in a previous  video as well  people can only remember between like  five and nine  things at the same time so if your  parameter list  is longer than let's say seven  like in the middle of between uh five  and nine more or less seven is what  people can remember so if you put  more in a parameter list people will not  be able to remember everything in one go  causing this back and forth behavior  looking up the definition  before they can continue with calling  the  function code clones or code duplication  we talked about that right where you  have code  across a code base that that's kind of  similar where you have  a method and somewhere else you have a  slightly different method like here's an  example  i have a a function called foo but i  also have a function called  goo that is slightly different  this is a very very different  cognitive issue so what happens here  is your brain wants to save energy  your brain you might be aware of this  your brain is a big big source of  or drain actually of energy so what your  brain  always wants to do is pattern match it  doesn't want to read all the tiny  details it doesn't want to  spell out in goo all the letters and all  the symbols  it wants to quickly recognize something  you might remember in a previous video  where we had  you remember sentences so this sentence  in a real language that i made up is  very very very hard to remember  but a sentence in a more normal  language it's not really a language but  at least the letters are familiar  what happens here is your brain now  starts to try to create words so if you  look at it maybe in this final word  maybe you just see  bar and not g bar because bar is a word  your brain is now really trying to make  sense of this  because it's one it wants to create  patterns  and if you present something to your  brain that's very very familiar  like actual sentences then rather than  reading c-a-t-l-o-v  yes your brain's just like cat glass  cake  so they're sort of like corner cutting  here  and that corner cutting that is what  makes  code clones so dangerous because we know  you saw this also in the visualization  of the memory system  that your long-term memory supports your  short-term memory  your short-term memory remembers  something and then your long-term memory  is hey  here's some related info this is what  you might be looking at  and that process here where your  long-term memory is  trying to be helpful actually works  against you  if you have code duplication because  what happens if you've seen  many foods in your lifetime very often  you've seen this function  foo that returns j plus plus  your brain sees a goo and it thinks oh i  know this guy  this is a foo and it's gonna return j  plus plus  it doesn't want to read all the details  it wants to make assumptions because  assumptions from long-term memory are  very quick  and efficient very much more efficient  than spelling out all the individual  letters  so the code smell of code duplication  misactivates so to say a very very  different part of your brain  because it's your long-term memory that  gives you information  that isn't helpful it's sort of like  negative help  because if you would read this in detail  if you would really  read letter by letter you would not  assume these two  snippets are the same but you don't want  to read code  letter by letter because in most other  situations  that isn't necessary and that's even  like contra  productive so that is the issue here  where your brain tries to chunk tries to  divide information up in  easy digestible pieces but in its effort  of trying to be helpful  it's actually if it's too helpful and  then it goes wrong  so you see that once you understand a  little bit about these cognitive systems  in your brain  you can look at code smells through a  different lens  and these were just a few examples but  in the book we go into way more detail  about different types of code smells and  how they relate  to underlying cognitive processes  so there's one thing i want to talk  about more  and that's the reason why a different  reason  why all smells are bad so as i said  every code smell has its own issues  all code smells create some sort of  cognitive issue whether in the  short-term memory or in the long-term  memory  or in the working memory but then  there's this additional reason  that all code smells are bad for  cognition  that again has to do with this three  different types of cognitive loads  that i want to zoom in a little bit more  before we end the video  so again consider these three different  types of cognitive load you have  intrinsic load that's just  how difficult the problem is you have  extraneous load that's  extra load added by the phrasing of the  problem so that's the type of load that  code smells might increase  unnecessarily and then you have this  third type of load  germain load that's the load that's  needed to store  something in your long-term memory and  apart from code duplication where your  long-term memory isn't so helpful  in general having a good long-term  memory for  for your code base actually is very  helpful because if you're searching for  information then you don't want to  go through the entire code base finding  something it's really nice if you have a  good long-term memory  for your specific code base so you can  quickly find  information you can quickly gain a  mental model of the code  so as i already said this middle part  is that the the part that code smells  might increase  that might make extra effort because  it's  a different phrasing a different form of  saying something  it could be the case if your code smell  is so bad  that there's so much extraneous load  that there's no more german load so in  the situation  where there's a bit of intrinsic load  and all the rest of your brain  is filled with oh my god what's going on  here oh oh what this is a  long message chain for example oh this  calls this oh this calls this oh this  calls this  then you're very very engaged in  understanding  the problem and we know from cognitive  science  that if your memory working memory is  working really really hard  then this will hamper your ability to  remember  things later there's this paper by  john sweller who is one of the pioneers  of cognitive load theory  in which he compared two groups of kids  so these are kids  learning math and the two groups 