d that  ultimately also got the right answers it  just took them more time  didn't have any green didn't have any  german rooms in their brains left  tying this back to code smells is that  if you are engaging with very very  complicated code  your brain is like the brains of those  kids in the study  where there's a bit of intrinsic load a  bit of yeah a bit of red  and everything else is blue it takes you  much more effort to read to dive into to  understand  code with code smells and my book  actually goes into  into depth also how we exactly measure  cognitive load and  how we know that code with code smells  actually  takes more effort to read they then lots  and lots of  cool experiments where people were like  headbands where you can measure brain  waves while people  read smelly code lots more detail about  that in the book if you're interested to  check that out  so we know scientifically that code  smells  create more cognitive load in the brain  it is very likely that that is the case  that there's  less or in some cases no germane  load left and what happens and i am  i have fallen victim to this many times  where i was reading very complicated  code and i was very much  in the code right i was like oh this  goes there okay this is the name of that  parameter oh this class oh this  api call and i managed to understand  the code in a brief moment of time i was  like okay i get it now  now i can fix this one bug okay now i  can add this one feature okay  i have everything it's right there  and then you step away you you bike home  from the office  like before grovid or you you go for  like a coffee  and then you come back to the code maybe  like the day after or like an hour after  after a meeting  and you're like i don't remember  anything i know i can look at kit hub  and i can see that i am the one that put  in this feature yesterday  i have no recollection of what i did  if you've ever been in such a situation  that was because you had  too little germ